oper would normally go  to discover and interact with rest apis  in order to understand how they work  in graphql the portal actually is a  built-in playground that accommodates  development it's almost like a built-in  ide  and so it allows you to explore new  queries on the fly and use things like  tab completion in order to discover  those queries in order to  learn how to use them better and so  we're not here to talk about whether you  should use graphql or rest  our philosophy is that you should use  graphql and rest  and i think one of the best ways to do  that is to take some of our rest-based  practices and apply them to graphql  this is something that we've seen in the  market this is something that we really  see working really well in a number of  customers and so we wanted to bring  those to you  so that you could leverage them and  understand what those are  the basis of this philosophy is that  apis are digital products  apis are a way for an enterprise to take  its assets and put it in the hands of  developers  whether those developers are internal  employees partners  or external customers in order to grow  the company's leverage of those assets  because apis are digital products we  rely on developers having a consistent  experience  in order to understand how to use those  digital products and build the  compelling experiences  they need in order to bring your assets  to market  and so consistency is incredibly  important because developer friction  is a is a huge challenge in  adoption of apis and in growth  strategies of digital companies  consistency in graphql really starts  with well-formed graphql and i think we  can learn a lot about that from  well-formed rest  you would never expect a well-formed  rest query to be list employees by  department  that sounds like a java function a  well-conformed rest query would be  a resource right get employees and you  pass employees id  and so that is what we consider wealth  and rest right nouns and those nouns are  plural graph hierarchy is fundamentally  a data-driven hierarchy  right so why do we frequently treat it  like a functional hierarchy  well-formed graphql shouldn't look like  the java functions which it frequently  does  well form graphql should look like  well-formed rest  why not use books and titles and authors  and treat this graphical hierarchy  the way it should be treated as opposed  to treating it as a functional hierarchy  and so the first recommendation we have  is that you should be data-oriented  a graph hierarchy is fundamentally a  data-driven hierarchy  be data-driven treat it that way don't  be so function-oriented  the second recommendation has to do with  submitting and retrieving data  those are not always mirrored operations  we frequently see and rest  leverage of cqrs right we see retrieving  data from multiple services in a  microservice architecture  being completely separate from how to  submit that data  and so as we think about how to do this  in graphql  we specifically think that mutations the  way a graphql developer submits data  can get very complex very quickly  especially when there's a lot of  different types  and especially when submitting very  little data and so  don't be afraid of cqrs many of you have  heard of cqrs which is command query  responsibility segregation  it's a design pattern that was first  identified by martin fowler  and fundamentally it's just a different  model to update information than the  model you use to read information  and so we're seeing in graphql programs  especially in large enterprises  especially those enterprises that  already have rest is that you get the  advantage  of using a rest-based front-end by  retrieving the data with graphql  but leveraging your existing rest apis  in order to submit that data to your  backend  the third pattern i'd like to talk about  is optimizing for reusability  you know in rest we think a lot about  our url taxonomy pagination filtering  and creating consistency  because developers can then predict what  the behavior will be of an api call  a developer sees the rest api and goes  aha i know how to interact with this  well in graphql we should be doing the  same thing  the challenge in large enterprise  graphql deployments  is that when it comes to schema  stitching frequently different business  units different lines of business  are developing different aspects to the  schema and then stitching that together  in order to look like  one comprehensive representation one big  graph to hand to an external developer  the behavior that json returned though  is frequently different  because there's no consistency it wasn't  built as an api product  as part of a developer program it was  built as an internal api  sort of a functional way of interacting  between components so when we think  about relay cursor connections  and input hints all the uniform behavior  that you would expect in a rest api  should  also exist in the graphql api so in  graphql we should be very careful about  optimizing for reusability  and avoiding situations where the  developer is confused or otherwise  doesn't understand  why the behavior is different in one  part of the schema  than it is in another part of the schema  and this is particularly a problem with  mutations  because uh if you submit data to one  part of the schema one way  and it gets recorded one way you don't  always realize that when you submit it  in another part of the schema  it got recorded another way so mutations  are even a larger  uh a larger surface area of challenges  when it comes to  optimizing for reusability and api  prioritization and so we particularly be  afraid there  uh be particularly conscientious  of the way you develop and design  mutations in graphql  lastly i want to talk about field names  because this is something that miguel  and i have experienced personally  it is developer hostile to provide  different data and behavior  for field names with the same name in  different parts of the schema  so if there is a name field in one part  of the schema  that accepts first middle last name and  then there's a name field and another  part of the schema  that expects uh last name dot first name  that is developer hostile  you are not treating our developers the  way they expect to be requested  the way they expect to be treated and  your developers will free  you will frequently experience developer  abandonment because your developers will  struggle  to adopt your graphql api so avoid the  graphical anti-patterns  treat the graph as a data-driven  hierarchy don't force graphql use rest  where and when it makes sense  and lastly optimize for reusability  think about api products think about the  developer experience and treating the  developer  as the customer they are because you  want your graphql api  to be used and you want it to get to  market  there are challenges in graphql  deployments and i'm going to go over  these challenges very quickly because i  think we're all somewhat familiar with  them  often z authorization authentication is  a huge challenge  as you know the graphql spec delegates  authenzy to the business logic layer  which means that it's typically not  handled  by the graphql server throttling is a  challenge there's no standard like we  see in api productization  for creating different levels and  different tiers of service  and different access to different api  developers  the third is analytics there's no  unified view of analytics which means  leveraging apis as products raising apis  to the level of business analytics as  opposed to just operational metrics is  not something that's  possible and lastly versioning ops we  don't understand  how versioning works in graphql and how  particularly  to address the need for clients using  different versions of different schemas  in order to implement their applications  so now you know what to do how do you do  it  for that i'd like to intro