icy that checks  are you allowed to make you know the  number of calls you're making making so  far  and finally we have the fine grain  authorization policy  that checks whether or not the current  token has the correct level of access  for the fields  within the specific query or the  specific mutation  that's being sent to the graphql server  let's take a look at the quota policy  you can see this policy has been  configured with a default value of  five calls per minute this is in fact  the behavior you saw initially when i  was making those first calls to get the  list of resources  later once i created an app and  subscribed my app to the  bronze or the graphql bronze api product  i was able to make more calls  and that's defined outside that's  defined in the product itself  so if we look at the graphql bronze  product  you can see that the graphql browse  product has defined a quota of  30 requests per minute  next let's look at how the fine grain  authorization was working  here you can see in the graphql bronze  product that i have defined a set of  allowed auth scopes these are  introspection and reader scope  i also have the other product which is  the graphql platinum  which has an even bigger set of oscopes  in addition to introspection and reader  scope it's got resort admin resort  manager and site admin  remember we use site admin when we went  when we went to create the new resort  so this is great you can see how you're  able to manage both the  sla the throttling level and the fine  grain authorization to  the graphql endpoint all within apigee  using the concept of api products  now you may be asking yourself how are  these scopes being translated to  individual  fields in the graphql schema let's take  a look at that next  all right so how do we go from a scope  to an actual set of fields or an  entitlement  to do that we have stored the  configuration of a mapping of  scopes to fields in apigee itself and  let's take a look at that this is in 